OK, This "hybrid" idea got me thinking. Probably thinking a bit too
much, but it just sounded like too much fun not to mention here.

Imagine if there was a whole ecosystem of puddings, accessible only by
using the "merge" discussed in previous posts to breed hybrid puddings.
Magical means (ie wishing for figurines, corpses & reverse geno) would
be allowed, but controlled to avoid abuse. More on that later.

To make the process worthwhile and entertaining, certain (hard to
attain) pudding types might confer intrinsics, and certain (very very
hard to attain) pudding types could grant wishes. To make it suitably
challenging, the whole process of pudding hybridisation would encourage
the player to split, heal and merge puddings recklessly which, as
described in previous posts, is a recipe for disaster as they start
splitting and merging beyond your control...

Here's sort of what I had in mind:

Dark Brown Pudding:
*******************
When a black pudding and brown pudding merge, the larger of the two
(determined by HP) devours the smaller completely. Therefore a merge
between a medium brown pud and a small black pud results in a large
brown pud. If the black pudding is the larger, the result is a black
pudding.
However, if the two puddings are of equal size (ie identical HP) then
there is only a 40% chance each of a black or brown pudding. The
remaining 20% results in a "dark brown pudding." This beast has the
same appearance as a brown pudding (looks harmless at first glance) but
has a bite almost as deadly that of it's other 'parent'. Merges between
dark brown an brown/black puddings result in brown/black puddings
respectively.

Light brown pudding:
********************
When a dark brown pudding is divided, it usually splits into a brown
and a black pudding. However, if it happens to be extremely weak (10-12
HP) then there is a small (20%) chance that one or both of the
offspring will be a light brown pudding. This pudding is necessarily
very small (initially 5-6 MaxHP) at creation but with very careful
splitting/ healing/ merging can be grown into a large (and reasonably
dangerous) monster. Note that light brown puddings are unlike their
photecessors in that they are damaged by metallic weapons and only
split by wooden ones. They also merge readily with other puddings
(higher chance of merges with adjacent puddings), so care must be taken
to quickly isolate light brown puddings if you wish to tinker further,
since light brown merges with black/ brown/ dark brown puddings result
in black/brown/brown puddings respectively.

Yellow pudding:
***************
To create a yellow pudding, you have to split a large (HP >=100) light
brown pudding. This will result in a tiny light brown pudding
(5-6MaxHP) and a large yellow pudding. Yellow puddings have the usual
acidic bite and very pointy teeth of all the puddings described so far,
but they are especially dangerous because they regenerate quickly and
merge aggressively. Almost any merge involving a yellow pudding results
in the yellow losing its identity altogether, heroicly donating its
(considerable- they regenerate, remember) HP to the other pudding. The
most notable exception is a yellow/ black pudding merge, which results
in orange puddings. Strangely, yellow puddings refuse to merge with
light brown puddings.

Golden Pudding:
***************
While the field of pudding psychiatry is as yet a young science,
remarkable progress has been made in the treatment of self-image
problems in puddings. Yellow puddings, in particular, often suffer from
a terrible lack of self-confidence, possibly stemming from their being
nothing but huge, brainless, mobile, blob-like killing machines. Such
psychological problems can be remedied, however, by the administration
of certain psychotropic substances, which free the patient's mind from
the cycle of self-loathing and despair that make them so aggressive. In
other words, smashing a !oHallu over a yellow pudding will turn it into
a golden pudding.
Golden puddings are tranquil, peaceful creatures that don't bite (even
when really big) and don't divide under any circumstances. They can be
troublesome though, because when they merge with other puddings the
results are (with a few notable exceptions) entirely unpredictable.
Except where stated otherwise, merges with other types of pudding
result in a randomly selected species from this list, with the
exception of dream, metallic, stone and zombie puddings.
Golden pudding corpses are, naturally, hallucinogenic, but they do
imbue the diner with a small boost in magic power.

Orange Pudding:
***************
A large, slow moving pudding. Its bite doesn't really hurt much and has
no acidic attack, but it can put the victim to sleep. Orange pudding
corpses are poisonous, but convey sleep resistance half the time.
Despite this, they are not sleep-resistant themselves, and although
they can't be divided by normal attacks, they sometimes split when sent
to sleep. They only merge reluctantly, but when they do, it goes as
follows:
Orange+ brown /light brown = yellow
Orange+ dark brown = brown
Orange+ black = red
Orange+ Red= 50%red, 50%orange

Red Puddings:
*************
A fairly unremarkable pudding, much like a yellow pudding except
slightly tougher, and sleep resistant. A red pudding merged with a
brown or dark brown pudding has a 50% chance of creating a hot pudding,
and a 50% chance of creating light brown pudding. Red+blue=2% purple,
98% sickly. Red+yellow=orange. red+(light/dark) brown/ black=red.
red+orange=50% red, 50% orange.

Hot Puddings:
*************
This beastie is fire-breathing, fire-resistant and its corpse
accasionally confers fire-resistance. It can be divided by fire attacks
or by metallic weapons, and takes extra damage from cold attacks. It
will merge happily with pretty much any other pudding. Merges with
black -> dark brown. Merges with dark brown->40% dark brown, 40% brown,
20% hot. Light brown/red -> hot. Orange->20% orange, 80% hot.
hot+yellow=60% Hot, 20% orange, 20% red.

Blue pudding:
*************
Like Hot puddings, but susbstitute "fire" for "cold". The easiest way
to create a blue pudding is to confuse a hot pudding. Will merge as
follows:
Blue+black/(light/dark) brown-> black/(light/dark) brown respectively.
Blue+yellow->80% green pudding, 20% acid blob.
Blue+orange->Sickly pudding.
Blue+hot->Purple Pudding.
Blue+Red=2% purple, 98% sickly.

Purple Pudding:
***************
The purple pudding is a tricky little so and so, because it has
intrinsic teleportitis. One minute it's there, next minute it's not.
Conveys that intrinsic to anyone who can catch and eat one. A potion of
gain level will upgrade a purple pudding to a royal pudding. Merge:
Purple+hot=Blue
Purple+blue=hot.
Purple+green/light brown/orange=sickly
Purple+dark brown, brown, black=black

Green Pudding:
**************
Fairly unremarkable, much like a black pudding except for the colour
(obviously) and that its bite and corpse are poisonous as well as
acidic. Oh, and that it can only be split by wooden weapons. Corpse
conveys poison resistance. Most merges will create sickly puddings,
unless stated otherwise.

Silver pudding:
***************
This one is very annoying. It's kind of like a fast-moving, mobile
version of the golden pudding. It regenerates quickly, splits
spontaneously at an alarming rate and merges with anything it can find,
messing up all your carefully laid plans. It also has reflection, which
can make things interesting if you happen to have a blue or hot pudding
hanging about as well. A confused silver pudding has a 10% chance of
becoming a metallic pudding.
Silver+yellow/royal=Golden pudding.
Silver+Gold=metallic pudding.
Any other combination: random result (but not dream pudding)

Sickly Pudding:
***************
Slow, weak, easily blocked and easily killed, the only real danger
posed by these unfortunate creatures is their infectious bite. Be
careful not to confuse their equally infectious corpses with those of
their cousin, the green pudding.
Sickly puddings will merge with other puddings. Because of the nature
of the sickly pudding's infection, the result of the merge is always
(with one or two exceptions, noted elsewhere) a sickly pudding. Also,
they are generated confused, so it is likely to bite another pudding
and if it does so it will pass the infection on, converting the other
pudding to sickly.

Metallic pudding:
*****************
Very well armoured and dangerous pudding this one, with built-in
reflection to boot. Fortunately it is slow moving and non-acidic.
Instead of a corpse it leaves behind one helmet (random 50% orcish/ 30%
dwarven/ 10% normal / 10% magic) per 50 MaxHP. Can only split
spontaneously or by artifact weapons, but any metal weapon used against
it will be dulled (lose 1 enchantment). The metallic pudding can only
merge with Gold or Silver puddings, which simply make the metallic
pudding bigger and uglier.

Pudding Zombie:
***************
Pudding zombies are slow and weak and do not split. However if they
merge with any other pudding, the result is a death pudding.

Stone Pudding:
**************
Very very slow moving, tough, rock-eating puddings. Non-acidic, they do
not split spontaneously, but can be broken into multiple puddings
(possibly more than two) with any crushing weapon (club, mace, force
bolt etc). Rather than corpses they leave piles of rocks. Stone
puddings can only merge with gold and silver puddings, which will
result in a  metallic pudding. Casting stone to flesh on a stone
pudding gives you a black pudding 50% of the time or a pile of tripe
rations otherwise.

Death Pudding:
**************
The dreaded pudding of instadeath. That's right, these beasties will
kill instantly with every bite that gets through. The bite is fatal to
both the biter and bitee.  Fortunately, they are slow moving, they
don't bite too hard (decent AC will stop them) and they don't divide.
If any pudding tries to merge with a death pudding then both are
killed, leaving behind two death pudding corpses. Don't eat a death
pudding corpse. Just don't.

Royal Pudding:
**************
The king of puddings! This purple creature is powerful and dangerous.
It hits hard, replenishes its health quickly, and moves rapidly. It can
only divide spontaneously (ie not from player attacks) and only then
when it has >99 hp. Remember that larger puddings are more likely to
divide. The result is a strong royal pudding (keeps 95% of hitpoints),
and a comparatively tiny, weak, silver pudding (remaining 5%). It very
rarely merges with other puddings, but when it does:
Royal+Black/dark brown/sickly=death pudding.
Royal+red=Hot
Royal+brown/ light brown=yellow.
Royal+hot/green/blue/orange/purple=bigger royal.
Royal+Golden=50% dream pudding, 50% silver.

Dream pudding:
**************
This fast, elusive creature is generated with one HP and a maxHP of
one. If it merges with another Dream pudding, it will still have MaxHP
of one. Therefore it is impossible to split them. They are always
generated scared. As though aware of their fragility, these tiny
puddings will actively seek out other puddings and merge with the first
one they find, becoming utterly absorbed by the other pudding. The
other pudding will retain its own type, it simply gains a little extra
mass (HP) from the merger.

However, if you can kill the white pudding before the kamikaze dash is
successful, the resulting corpse will grant a wish. Naturally, dream
puddings are highly sought-after, but if you go back and look, you'll
see that it takes quite a lot of work to get one.

First you need to somehow get a black and brown pudding together. Now
you need to split and heal them until they have equal HP, then wait
around for them to merge and create a dark brown pudding. Now you have
to isolate it from other puddings to prevent merging, while keeping at
least one spare black and one brown pudding alive for later. Now
attack/heal the dark brown pudding to get it to 10-12 HP and split it
for the 20% chance of a feeble light brown pudding. Now you have to get
this one from 6HP up to a hundred or more- either by split/heal/merge
or by creating a lot more light brown puddings and merging them. Again,
you have to be careful not to lose your light brown to a merge with a
brown/black pudding. Bear in mind that while you're doing all this, all
your "spare" puddings are dividing, merging and healing to get bigger
and uglier all the time...

Now you can split your light brown for a yellow. You could hallucinate
it in the hopes of generating something more interesting and skipping a
step, but this is unlikely. The more reliable method is to merge your
yellow with a black for an orange, and then another black for a red,
then a brown for a (50% chance of a) hot. Now confuse the hot to make a
blue, and somehow guide it  over to a spare hot or red (you did create
a spare hot with your wand of fire, didn't you?) to create a purple,
which you can upgrade to royal using a potion of gain level. All this
without the puddings you actually need merging with something else to
become something you don't want. And without any of them or some stray
monster killing you. And still, all those spare puddings are squidging
about merging, splitting and regenerating- especially your spare
yellow, which has quick regeneration and aggressive merging, remember,
but which you'll need to turn golden in a minute for the final merger.
Where is it? It's just over there, I'll go and hit it with
hallucination and- oh no, my royal just paired up with that spare brown
I was saving...

You get the idea. Personally, I think anyone capable of getting through
that little lot unscathed either fully deserves or doesn't particularly
need the wish at the end of it. Mostly it would be for fun, although I
can't imagine too many unspoiled players stumbling into it without some
pretty heavy cookie-hints.

Oh, and there are probably a few pudding merge combos I haven't covered
there, but I think I have most of them. If I'd been smart I'd have made
a table. Oh well.

Closing the loopholes:
**********************
I can see a small number of loopholes here which might allow players to
short-cut the tortuous process described above. I think they can all be
closed satisfactorily.

First up, wishing: You can wish for a figurine/ statue of a pudding and
animate it. This isn't a problem, since really you're just exchanging a
wish for another wish. Ok, there are other benefits to pudding
hybridisation, but there are risks too, and you might as well get
*something* for your wish.

Undead turning: You could restrict unauthorised breeding of puddings by
storing them as corpses, then reviving them when they are needed. To
counter this, pudding corpses zapped with UT should come back as
pudding zombies (see above). Nyer.

Petrification: Similar to the UT trick above, petrification should
produce "stone puddings" rather than "a statue of a <foo> pudding." I
believe most puddings are too acidic to petrify anyway.

Reverse genocide: Tricky this one, since a RG for dream puddings should
theoretically spawn a roomfull of wishes. Instead I would suggest that
any RG for a specific type of pudding generates a handful of the
desired type, surrounded by a heap more of various random types
(particularly sickly, death, metallic, silver). This gives the dream
puddings a chance to escaoe by hiding behind something with reflection
or merging with something else, and also increases the risk to the
player of facing an uncontrolled pudding explosion. This is a bit
kludgy, but less so than forbidding white-pudding RGs altogether.

So, there it is then. Sound like fun? Who wants to code it? =-)


